Not in smoking, drinking, lying, swearing, or otherwise aping the vices of men, do we act the manly part, but in living above reproach, in avoiding evil companions and resisting all temptations to do wrong;

in honoring and obeying our parents, and striving to do the will of God;

in all this do we best fit ourselves for lives of usefulness upon the earth, and for the duties and responsibilities of manhood.

We read on the cover of the Contributor that "The society of the future is what we make it;"let us not forget that on our individual efforts depend not only our own lives, but in a great measure the history of the world for we have an influence, and can use it for good or evil;

let us use it for good every day of our lives, that the society of the future, as far as we are concerned in the forming of it, may be all that could be desired, and the world the better that we have lived.

Then in old age we shall not be ashamed of our youth, but may be able to look back on a life well spent, and look forward to a glory and exaltation with our Heavenly Father through life eternal.

George Manwaring.
